Z||000000||MontanaFiero||08-30-2001||04:13 AM||||<I>If you have put a camaro 3.4 in your fiero, you know what I am having trouble with...</I><BR>I attempted to remove the oil filter adapter and just ended up putting it back on again because it doesn't seem to be in the way and there was no way to screw a filter on there anyway. It has the camaro <u>Oil Pressure Sender</u> attached to it.   <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/oilsendq/oilsend.jpg">  <P>I am noting here below that this sending unit has <u>three</u> terminals just like the Fiero's original sending unit in the third picture. I am also noting below that if you remove the plug (circled) the threading is wrong and requires the "search" for an adapter to make the stock oil sender setup attach properly.   <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/oilsendq/oilsend1.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/oilsendq/fierooilsend.jpg">  <P>Below is a picture of the camaro wire connector for the camaro oil sender. It only has <u>one</u> wire leading from it, yet the <u>plug</u> is set up for <u>three</u>. Am I to assume that the other two terminals serve as the fuel pump backup switch like the ones on the Fiero do? If I used the plug off of the camaro system and integrated it into the Fiero system can I assume that the unit and system will function the same? What about clearance between the firewall and the motor? I am wondering if any of you with this conversion motor have tried this. Any tips?    <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/oilsendq/singlewire.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by MontanaFiero (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||3
